{"kind": "authoring.reviewed", "at": "2026-09-13T23:56:34.709768+00:00", "summary": "0 outcome(s) added, 4 checkpoint concern(s)", "applied": [], "rejected": [], "concerns": ["s1: The checkpoint is an OR whose second branch only requires a button named \"Search\" to be present. A Search button exists on almost every screen of a back-office banking app (transaction search, account search, global nav search). If the URL assertion fails because the app landed somewhere else, the button branch will silently rescue the step and the flow will type a member id into whatever search box happens to be on screen. Tighten to the URL check alone, or pair the button with a heading unique to Member Search.", "s2: No checkpoint at all. Nothing confirms the member id was actually typed into the member-id field rather than being dropped, truncated, or routed to a different input. If the field ends up empty the subsequent search returns the unfiltered member list, step s3's URL-only checkpoint still passes, and step s4 opens an arbitrary member's record \u2014 producing a confidently wrong savings balance. Add a value assertion on the member-id input.", "s3: The checkpoint only asserts the URL is /members/search. That URL is reached identically for a hit, for the 'No records found matching your search criteria.' case, and plausibly for a validation rejection, so the checkpoint cannot distinguish success from the business outcome. It must be paired with outcome detection on the results region \u2014 otherwise the not-found answer is only discovered later, as an unexplained failure at s4.", "s4: This is where a legitimate MEMBER_NOT_FOUND surfaces as a hard automation failure: with no results there is no record to open, the URL never matches /members/<digits>, and the run aborts with a checkpoint error rather than returning the business answer. The not-found alert must be evaluated before this checkpoint is enforced. Separately, the URL pattern alone is weak evidence that the correct member was opened and that balances rendered \u2014 it would pass on any member detail page, including the wrong one; assert the member id or a savings-balance label on the page."]}
